==History==
{{Main|Wikipedia:Core content policies}}

"Neutral Point Of View" is one of the oldest governing concepts on Wikipedia. Originally appearing within [[Nupedia]] titled "[https://web.archive.org/web/20001205000200/http://www.nupedia.com/policy.shtml#III Non-bias policy]", it was drafted by [[Larry Sanger]] in 2000. In 2001, Sanger suggested that avoiding bias as one of Wikipedia's [https://web.archive.org/web/20010416035716/www.wikipedia.com/wiki/RulesToConsider "rules to consider"]. This [http://cyber.law.harvard.edu/~reagle/wp-redux/NeutralPointOfView/982358834.html was codified] with the objective of the NPOV policy to produce an unbiased encyclopedia. The [[Special:PermanentLink/270453|original NPOV policy statement on Wikipedia]] was added by Sanger on December 26, 2001. [[Jimmy Wales]] has qualified NPOV as "non-negotiable", consistently, throughout various discussions: [https://web.archive.org/web/20010416035757/http://www.wikipedia.com/wiki/NeutralPointOfView 2001 statement], [https://web.archive.org/web/20110406110157/http://lists.wikimedia.org/pipermail/wikien-l/2003-November/008096.html November 2003], [http://lists.wikimedia.org/pipermail/wikien-l/2006-April/044386.html April 2006], [https://web.archive.org/web/20110217075343/http://www.gossamer-threads.com/lists/wiki/foundation/123928#123928 March 2008].

[[Wikipedia:No original research|No original research]] (NOR) and [[Wikipedia:Verifiability|verifiability]] (V) have their origins in the NPOV policy and the problem of dealing with [[#Due and undue weight|undue weight]] and [[Wikipedia:Fringe theories|fringe theories]]. The [[Special:PermanentLink/2014449|NOR policy was established in 2003]] to address problematic uses of sources. The [[Special:PermanentLink/1339095|verifiability policy was established in 2003]] to ensure the accuracy of articles by encouraging editors to cite sources. Development of the undue-weight section also started in 2003, for which a [http://mail.wikipedia.org/pipermail/wikien-l/2003-September/006715.html mailing-list post] by Jimmy Wales in September was instrumental.

